### TITLE ###
CursorMovesThroughTheTextWithoutShiftingIt
### DESCRIPTION ###
The editor uses the terminal's own cursor, not the glyph the prompts draw, so
the text stays put as the cursor moves. ScriptDriver marks the position with a
caret; read FRAMES and confirm only the caret moves between them.
### PROMPT ###
>
### KEYS ###
"abc"
left
left
ctrl-e
ctrl-a
enter
### VALUE ###
abc
### OUTCOME ###
submitted
### FRAMES ###
--- frame 0 (initial) ---
>‸
--- frame 1 (a) ---
>a‸
--- frame 2 (b) ---
>ab‸
--- frame 3 (c) ---
>abc‸
--- frame 4 (left) ---
>ab‸c
--- frame 5 (left) ---
>a‸bc
--- frame 6 (ctrl-e) ---
>abc‸
--- frame 7 (ctrl-a) ---
>‸abc
--- frame 8 (enter) ---
>abc

### TITLE ###
KillAndWordDeleteEditFromTheCursor
### DESCRIPTION ###
Ctrl-K cuts to end of line, Ctrl-W eats the word behind the cursor, and Ctrl-U
clears the line - the readline chords radish previously dropped at the byte
level.
### PROMPT ###
>
### KEYS ###
"hello world"
ctrl-w
"there"
ctrl-a
ctrl-k
"done"
enter
### VALUE ###
done
### OUTCOME ###
submitted
### FRAMES ###
